1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,560
In 2013, Microsoft did something
unusual for a software giant.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:09,440
It wrote a YouTube app for its own phones,
because Google wouldn't.

3
00:00:09,933 --> 00:00:14,713
Microsoft's version didn't show Google's
adverts, and Google soon wanted it gone.

4
00:00:15,019 --> 00:00:17,899
So Microsoft rebuilt it, adverts and all.

5
00:00:18,679 --> 00:00:20,279
Google blocked that one too.

6
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:22,640
Think about that for a moment.

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,060
The biggest software company in the world

8
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:28,580
couldn't put the most popular video site
on its own phone.

9
00:00:29,274 --> 00:00:32,374
And Windows Phone
was not a half-hearted project.

10
00:00:32,834 --> 00:00:36,554
Microsoft had the money,
a design that reviewers loved,

11
00:00:36,654 --> 00:00:39,794
and Nokia, at the time
the biggest phone maker on Earth.

12
00:00:40,401 --> 00:00:43,601
At its best, in 2013,
about three smartphones

13
00:00:43,831 --> 00:00:46,621
in every hundred sold ran Windows Phone.

14
00:00:47,481 --> 00:00:51,281
In 2015, Microsoft wrote off
seven point six billion dollars

15
00:00:51,511 --> 00:00:54,641
on the phone business
it had bought from Nokia,

16
00:00:54,821 --> 00:00:58,201
and announced up to seven
thousand eight hundred job cuts.

17
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:01,584
Two years later, it quietly gave up.

18
00:01:02,601 --> 00:01:04,541
So what killed Windows Phone?

19
00:01:05,921 --> 00:01:09,581
Let's treat it like a case.
There are five suspects.

20
00:01:10,221 --> 00:01:13,061
Each one had a motive,
and some of them have an alibi.

21
00:01:15,261 --> 00:01:17,221
Suspect number one: time.

22
00:01:17,981 --> 00:01:22,841
In January 2007, Steve Ballmer,
Microsoft's chief executive,

23
00:01:23,181 --> 00:01:25,341
was asked about the brand-new iPhone.

24
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:26,960
He laughed at it.

25
00:01:27,190 --> 00:01:31,700
Five hundred dollars, even
with a phone contract, and no keyboard.

26
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:35,000
Business customers, he said,
wouldn't want it.

27
00:01:35,543 --> 00:01:38,003
That wasn't a silly view at the time.

28
00:01:38,283 --> 00:01:42,223
Microsoft had been selling
smartphone software since 2002,

29
00:01:42,563 --> 00:01:45,663
and people who did business on a phone
wanted a keyboard.

30
00:01:46,435 --> 00:01:49,675
The iPhone sold anyway,
and Android followed.

31
00:01:50,395 --> 00:01:53,855
Windows Phone 7 reached the shops
in October 2010,

32
00:01:53,935 --> 00:01:57,535
three years and nine months
after the iPhone was first shown.

33
00:01:58,139 --> 00:02:00,139
Guilty? Not so fast.

34
00:02:00,519 --> 00:02:02,079
Android was late too.

35
00:02:02,879 --> 00:02:05,159
The first Android phone came out
twenty-one months

36
00:02:05,389 --> 00:02:08,099
after the iPhone was shown,
and Android won.

37
00:02:08,513 --> 00:02:13,613
So being late can't be the whole story.
Keep it in mind, though. It comes back.

38
00:02:15,923 --> 00:02:18,223
Suspect number two: the phone itself.

39
00:02:19,055 --> 00:02:21,955
The first Windows Phone
had no copy and paste,

40
00:02:22,035 --> 00:02:25,675
and apps from other companies
couldn't keep running in the background.

41
00:02:26,062 --> 00:02:29,102
That sounds bad, until you remember
that the first iPhone

42
00:02:29,332 --> 00:02:33,822
had no copy and paste either,
and no app store for a whole year.

43
00:02:34,150 --> 00:02:35,810
And people liked the design.

44
00:02:36,630 --> 00:02:38,170
Big, clean type.

45
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:41,650
Square tiles on the home screen
that showed your messages,

46
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:45,010
your missed calls and your photos
without opening anything.

47
00:02:45,557 --> 00:02:47,317
Windows Phone wasn't perfect.

48
00:02:47,397 --> 00:02:49,417
But it wasn't a bad phone.

49
00:02:49,857 --> 00:02:51,457
And that distinction matters.

50
00:02:54,067 --> 00:02:57,747
Suspect number three,
and the one everybody names: the apps.

51
00:02:58,515 --> 00:03:01,775
Instagram came to Windows Phone
in late 2013,

52
00:03:01,855 --> 00:03:05,375
three years after the iPhone,
and labelled as a beta.

53
00:03:06,077 --> 00:03:08,017
Snapchat never came at all.

54
00:03:08,677 --> 00:03:13,357
And when people used unofficial Snapchat
apps instead, Snapchat blocked them.

55
00:03:13,877 --> 00:03:16,477
For many owners,
the gap was closer to home.

56
00:03:17,077 --> 00:03:18,617
Their bank had no app.

57
00:03:19,077 --> 00:03:20,977
Neither did their train company.

58
00:03:21,457 --> 00:03:23,997
And the game their friends
played wasn't there.

59
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:27,040
Microsoft knew. It paid developers.

60
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:29,100
It gave away phones.

61
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:33,120
In some cases,
its own engineers wrote the app for them.

62
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:36,720
So that's our answer, right?
No apps. Case closed.

63
00:03:37,528 --> 00:03:39,968
Not quite. "No apps" isn't a cause.

64
00:03:40,508 --> 00:03:42,128
It's the thing that needs explaining.

65
00:03:42,981 --> 00:03:47,821
Why would a developer skip a platform
from Microsoft, with Nokia behind it?

66
00:03:48,282 --> 00:03:50,442
Imagine you run a small studio.

67
00:03:51,122 --> 00:03:54,182
An app costs money to build,
and more to keep up to date.

68
00:03:55,042 --> 00:03:58,562
On a platform with eighty percent
of users, that money comes back.

69
00:03:59,002 --> 00:04:01,822
On one with three percent,
it usually doesn't.

70
00:04:02,293 --> 00:04:03,973
Where would you spend it?

71
00:04:04,851 --> 00:04:07,511
In 2017, a Microsoft vice president,

72
00:04:07,741 --> 00:04:11,631
long the public face of Windows Phone,
said much the same.

73
00:04:12,231 --> 00:04:15,411
They had paid developers,
they had written apps for them,

74
00:04:15,491 --> 00:04:18,611
but there were too few users
for most companies to invest.

75
00:04:19,170 --> 00:04:22,090
So the apps weren't there
because the users weren't there.

76
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:25,530
And the users weren't there
because the apps weren't.

77
00:04:26,009 --> 00:04:26,449
That's a loop.

78
00:04:26,679 --> 00:04:29,049
And a loop doesn't tell us who started it.

79
00:04:29,489 --> 00:04:30,829
Let's keep going.

80
00:04:33,219 --> 00:04:34,819
Suspect number four: Nokia.

81
00:04:35,049 --> 00:04:37,059
Or rather, one man at Nokia.

82
00:04:37,817 --> 00:04:41,817
In 2010, Nokia was still
the biggest phone maker in the world.

83
00:04:42,457 --> 00:04:45,957
Its smartphones ran its own system,
called Symbian,

84
00:04:46,037 --> 00:04:49,937
and Symbian was losing ground fast
to the iPhone and Android.

85
00:04:50,308 --> 00:04:53,568
That September,
Nokia hired a new boss, Stephen Elop.

86
00:04:53,648 --> 00:04:55,788
He came straight from Microsoft.

87
00:04:56,806 --> 00:05:00,126
In February 2011,
he sent his staff a memo.

88
00:05:00,746 --> 00:05:03,926
Nokia, he wrote,
was standing on a burning platform.

89
00:05:04,627 --> 00:05:06,407
Three days later, Nokia announced

90
00:05:06,637 --> 00:05:09,807
that its future smartphones
would run Windows Phone.

91
00:05:10,461 --> 00:05:13,081
The first of them arrived
nine months later.

92
00:05:13,921 --> 00:05:16,281
Until then,
Nokia kept selling Symbian phones,

93
00:05:16,511 --> 00:05:20,141
on a system Nokia had just told the world
it was leaving.

94
00:05:20,901 --> 00:05:22,801
Sales of those phones collapsed.

95
00:05:23,660 --> 00:05:27,330
Nokia even had a new system of its own,
called MeeGo.

96
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:30,840
Its only phone, the N9,
got excellent reviews.

97
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:33,400
Nokia dropped MeeGo anyway.

98
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:39,990
In 2013, Microsoft agreed to buy
Nokia's phone business, in a deal worth

99
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:44,710
about seven billion dollars,
and Elop went back to Microsoft with it.

100
00:05:45,215 --> 00:05:47,595
To a lot of people,
that looked like a plan.

101
00:05:48,315 --> 00:05:52,855
A Microsoft man walks into Nokia, ties
it to Windows, and hands it back cheap.

102
00:05:53,595 --> 00:05:54,855
A Trojan horse.

103
00:05:55,555 --> 00:05:57,655
There's no evidence it was a plan.

104
00:05:58,315 --> 00:05:59,895
But a bet doesn't have to be a conspiracy

105
00:06:00,125 --> 00:06:03,635
to be a costly mistake,
and for Nokia, it was.

106
00:06:04,936 --> 00:06:06,936
Still, did it kill Windows Phone?

107
00:06:07,356 --> 00:06:10,216
Because of that bet,
the biggest phone maker of 2011

108
00:06:10,446 --> 00:06:15,096
was making Windows phones, and reviewers
praised their cameras and design.

109
00:06:15,807 --> 00:06:18,867
And with all of that,
Windows Phone reached three percent.

110
00:06:19,901 --> 00:06:22,301
Nokia made a risky bet, and lost.

111
00:06:22,901 --> 00:06:26,121
But whatever was killing Windows Phone
was stronger than Nokia.

112
00:06:28,671 --> 00:06:30,751
Suspect number five: Google.

113
00:06:31,540 --> 00:06:33,320
We've seen the YouTube fight.

114
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:36,360
In 2012, a Google manager was asked

115
00:06:36,590 --> 00:06:39,520
why Google wasn't making its apps
for Windows Phone.

116
00:06:40,160 --> 00:06:41,840
His answer was simple.

117
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:45,360
Google would go where the users are,
and they weren't there.

118
00:06:45,957 --> 00:06:49,197
Google also gave Android
to phone makers for free.

119
00:06:50,257 --> 00:06:54,897
Microsoft charged them
a licence fee for every phone, until 2014.

120
00:06:55,804 --> 00:06:57,604
And here's the strange part.

121
00:06:57,984 --> 00:07:00,644
Android wasn't free for everyone.

122
00:07:01,084 --> 00:07:04,364
Microsoft held patents
that it said Android used,

123
00:07:04,544 --> 00:07:08,764
and most big Android phone makers
paid Microsoft for every phone they sold.

124
00:07:09,387 --> 00:07:13,627
In one year, Samsung alone paid Microsoft
about a billion dollars.

125
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:17,620
Microsoft was making good money
from its rival's success.

126
00:07:18,968 --> 00:07:21,048
So did Google kill Windows Phone?

127
00:07:21,588 --> 00:07:24,868
Look at its reason again.
Not enough users.

128
00:07:25,588 --> 00:07:28,428
That's the same reason
every other developer gave.

129
00:07:29,203 --> 00:07:31,043
Google didn't start the loop.

130
00:07:31,323 --> 00:07:35,383
But it had no reason to help a rival
break out of it, and it didn't.

131
00:07:37,513 --> 00:07:40,173
Five suspects,
and none of them quite did it.

132
00:07:40,553 --> 00:07:42,293
Which means we've missed one.

133
00:07:43,893 --> 00:07:46,073
Suspect number six: Microsoft.

134
00:07:47,619 --> 00:07:50,859
Before Windows Phone,
Microsoft had Windows Mobile,

135
00:07:51,079 --> 00:07:53,239
with thousands of apps written for it.

136
00:07:53,752 --> 00:07:56,432
Windows Phone 7 couldn't run any of them.

137
00:07:56,732 --> 00:08:00,212
Developers had to start again,
and rewrite their apps.

138
00:08:00,733 --> 00:08:05,313
Then, in April 2012,
the Lumia 900 went on sale in America.

139
00:08:06,073 --> 00:08:07,893
Nokia's big flagship.

140
00:08:08,468 --> 00:08:11,468
Ten weeks later,
Microsoft announced Windows Phone 8,

141
00:08:11,698 --> 00:08:15,008
and said the Lumia 900 would never get it.

142
00:08:15,519 --> 00:08:18,139
Nor would any other phone already on sale.

143
00:08:18,859 --> 00:08:21,259
Old apps would still run
on the new phones,

144
00:08:21,359 --> 00:08:23,759
but new apps wouldn't run on the old ones.

145
00:08:24,406 --> 00:08:28,506
This time, it was the users
who had to start again, with a new phone.

146
00:08:29,417 --> 00:08:34,777
In 2015, Windows 10 Mobile arrived,
with yet another way of building apps.

147
00:08:35,537 --> 00:08:38,657
Older apps still worked,
but new ones were increasingly

148
00:08:38,887 --> 00:08:42,737
written for the new system,
and many phones sold with Windows Phone 8

149
00:08:42,967 --> 00:08:44,517
were never offered the upgrade.

150
00:08:45,344 --> 00:08:47,344
Three resets in five years.

151
00:08:47,944 --> 00:08:49,804
Not every one broke everything.

152
00:08:50,524 --> 00:08:54,804
But each one told someone, users,
developers, or both, the same thing.

153
00:08:55,024 --> 00:08:57,444
Whatever you put in here, start again.

154
00:09:00,154 --> 00:09:04,154
To see why that was fatal,
we need to go back to 1999.

155
00:09:04,878 --> 00:09:07,838
That year, an American judge
wrote down why no rival

156
00:09:08,068 --> 00:09:09,958
could catch Windows on the PC.

157
00:09:10,777 --> 00:09:13,777
He called it the applications
barrier to entry.

158
00:09:14,478 --> 00:09:18,278
People bought Windows because the programs
they needed ran on it.

159
00:09:18,758 --> 00:09:22,418
Developers wrote for Windows
because that's where the people were.

160
00:09:23,003 --> 00:09:24,883
Any new system had to climb that wall

161
00:09:25,113 --> 00:09:28,703
from the outside,
with no programs and no people.

162
00:09:29,885 --> 00:09:32,945
We've looked at how that wall
made Windows on the PC.

163
00:09:33,465 --> 00:09:36,745
On phones, the same machine
ran the other way.

164
00:09:37,343 --> 00:09:41,463
By 2010, the loop was already spinning
for the iPhone and Android.

165
00:09:42,183 --> 00:09:45,323
This time,
Microsoft was the one outside the wall.

166
00:09:45,911 --> 00:09:47,751
That's why being late mattered.

167
00:09:48,551 --> 00:09:50,371
Every month, the wall got higher.

168
00:09:51,051 --> 00:09:53,011
Android had been late too,
but it started climbing

169
00:09:53,241 --> 00:09:56,651
while the wall was still low,
and it was free for phone makers.

170
00:09:57,181 --> 00:09:59,521
And that's why the resets hurt so much.

171
00:10:00,261 --> 00:10:03,241
On a wall like that,
you can't afford to stop climbing.

172
00:10:03,941 --> 00:10:08,501
Every reset gave users a reason to wait,
and developers a reason to hold off.

173
00:10:10,731 --> 00:10:12,731
So who killed Windows Phone?

174
00:10:14,115 --> 00:10:16,795
Not Nokia, on its own. Not Google.

175
00:10:16,875 --> 00:10:18,415
Not really the missing apps.

176
00:10:19,120 --> 00:10:22,160
It was the same wall
that made Windows untouchable.

177
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:25,360
Microsoft knew that wall
better than anyone.

178
00:10:25,880 --> 00:10:28,060
It had made its fortune on the inside.

179
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:33,580
On phones, it never found a way over,
and every reset made the climb harder.

180
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:39,780
In October 2017,
Microsoft said it would keep fixing

181
00:10:40,010 --> 00:10:42,960
Windows on phones,
but new features and new hardware

182
00:10:43,190 --> 00:10:44,960
were no longer the focus.

183
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:48,540
Support ended in December 2019.

184
00:10:49,275 --> 00:10:51,495
Microsoft didn't leave your phone, though.

185
00:10:51,795 --> 00:10:56,575
Outlook, Word, Excel and Teams
all run on the iPhone and on Android.

186
00:10:56,967 --> 00:11:01,527
If you can't get over the wall, you can
still sell things to the people inside it.

187
00:11:02,612 --> 00:11:04,412
So here's a question for you.

188
00:11:05,012 --> 00:11:07,472
Was there ever room
for a third kind of phone?

189
00:11:07,772 --> 00:11:10,352
Or was Windows Phone
doomed from the start?

190
00:11:10,812 --> 00:11:11,962
Let me know in the comments.
